review.raw:
VERDICT
Correct: your code finds a common multiple of 2 and n by multiplication in a loop, but it takes extra iterations and multiplications when a direct single-step check is all that is needed.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You initialized your candidate number and counter properly.

You checked both divisibility conditions (num % 2 != 0 and num % n != 0) with a while loop to ensure validity.

The code handles both odd and even values of n without crashing.

Is it correct?

Yes, it passes all LeetCode test cases because for odd n, num = n * 2 on the first pass, which immediately satisfies num % 2 == 0 and num % n == 0. For even n, the loop condition fails right away on the first check, returning n immediately.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There is unnecessary computation: num = num * count inside the loop will grow exponentially if the loop runs more than once, though here the loop runs at most once.

Edge cases handled properly: n = 1 (loop runs once, returns 2), n = 2 (loop condition is false initially, returns 2), and large even/odd numbers within the range 1 to 150.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(1) because the while loop runs at most once for any positive integer n.

Space complexity: O(1) because only two scalar integer variables (num and count) are used.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't:

If n is even, num % 2 == 0 is already true and num % n == 0 is true, so the while loop never runs and returns n. If n is odd, the loop runs once: num becomes n * 2, which is divisible by both 2 and n, so the loop exits and returns 2 * n.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Multiplicative Step Simulation (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Introductory trial-and-error simulation often written by beginners testing divisibility.

Idea:

Start with num = n. While num is not divisible by 2 or not divisible by n, multiply num by an incrementing counter.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int smallestEvenMultiple(int n) {
        int num = n;
        int count = 2;
        while (num % 2 != 0 || num % n != 0) {
            num = num * count;
            count++;
        }
        return num;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) because it terminates within one iteration.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of using an unbounded multiplication loop, we can test successive multiples of n (n, 2n, 3n...) linearly, which represents true brute-force search.

Approach 2: Linear Multiples Search (Brute Force)

Where it comes from:

Canonical brute force search found in LeetCode discussion forums and textbooks.

Idea:

Check multiples of n one by one: 1n, 2n, 3*n... The first multiple that is divisible by 2 is the answer.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int smallestEvenMultiple(int n) {
        int multiple = n;
        while (multiple % 2 != 0) {
            multiple += n;
        }
        return multiple;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) because it tests at most two values (n and 2*n).

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

A loop is unnecessary. Number theory tells us the smallest positive common multiple of 2 and n is the Least Common Multiple (LCM), which can be computed via GCD.

Approach 3: Greatest Common Divisor / LCM Formula

Where it comes from:

Standard Number Theory and C++ standard library std::lcm (or std::gcd from ).

Idea:

The smallest multiple of both 2 and n is by definition LCM(2, n). The mathematical relationship is LCM(a, b) = (a * b) / GCD(a, b). Since GCD(2, n) is 2 if n is even and 1 if n is odd, LCM directly yields n or 2*n.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <numeric>

class Solution {
public:
    int smallestEvenMultiple(int n) {
        return std::lcm(2, n);
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) since GCD between 2 and any number takes at most two Euclidean steps.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

Since one of the numbers is fixed at 2, we do not need the full LCM machinery; we can check parity directly with an if-else condition or ternary operator.

Approach 4: Parity Check (Conditional Branching / Ternary)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial Approach 1.

Idea:

If n is divisible by 2 (even), the smallest even multiple of n is n itself. If n is not divisible by 2 (odd), the smallest even multiple is n * 2.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int smallestEvenMultiple(int n) {
        if (n % 2 == 0) {
            return n;
        }
        return n * 2;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) for a single modulo check.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

We can eliminate the conditional branch entirely by using bitwise operations.

Approach 5: Bitwise Shift and Masking (Branchless Optimization)

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode community solutions and competitive programming bit-hacks.

Idea:

In binary, an odd number has its lowest bit (n & 1) equal to 1, while an even number has (n & 1) equal to 0. Multiplying by 2 is equivalent to a left bit-shift (n << 1). Shifting n by (n & 1), written as n << (n & 1), leaves an even number as n (shifted by 0) and doubles an odd number (shifted by 1).

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int smallestEvenMultiple(int n) {
        return n << (n & 1);
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) executing in one CPU instruction cycle.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used a variant of Approach 1 (Multiplicative Step Simulation).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 4 (the clean one-line ternary: return n % 2 == 0 ? n : n * 2;). Mentioning the bitwise trick (Approach 5) is a great bonus to showcase your computer architecture knowledge.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: How would you solve this if 2 was replaced by any arbitrary positive integer k (Smallest Common Multiple of n and k)?

Answer: Use the general Least Common Multiple formula: LCM(n, k) = (n / GCD(n, k)) * k. Dividing first prevents potential 32-bit integer overflow before multiplication.

Question 2: Why does n << (n & 1) work without an if condition?

Answer: For odd numbers, n & 1 evaluates to 1, so n is shifted left by 1 bit, which is multiplication by 2. For even numbers, n & 1 evaluates to 0, so n is shifted by 0 bits, leaving it unchanged.

Question 3: Can this computation ever overflow a 32-bit signed integer?

Answer: The problem constraint states n is up to 150. Even with arbitrary integers, n * 2 will only overflow if n > INT_MAX / 2.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Any even number is already its own smallest even multiple, while any odd number must be multiplied by 2.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Do not use a while loop when the decision depends purely on whether a number is even or odd.

Look at the math properties before writing a loop: divisibility by 2 is just parity (even vs. odd).

Simplify if-else logic into a concise ternary expression return (n % 2 == 0) ? n : 2 * n;.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Parity checking, Least Common Multiple (LCM), and bitwise bit-shifts.

THE BEST SOLUTION

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int smallestEvenMultiple(int n) {
        return (n % 2 == 0) ? n : (n * 2);
    }
};
